-- title:
--   Cone membership as an inner-product inequality
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space. For $v, h \in E$ and an angle $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$, membership of $h$ in the cone with axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta$ is characterised by
--   $$h \in \mathcal{C}(v,\vartheta) \iff h \neq 0 \ \text{ and } \ \cos\vartheta \cdot \|h\| < \langle v, h\rangle ,$$
--   where $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ is the real inner product and $\|\cdot\|$ the associated norm. In words: a nonzero vector lies in the cone exactly when its projection onto the axis exceeds $\cos\vartheta$ times its length — the strict form of the condition that the angle between $h$ and $v$ be less than $\vartheta$.
--
--   This is the working characterisation of cone membership: it removes all reference to angles and leaves an inequality between an inner product and a multiple of a norm, which is linear in $h$ on each ray and therefore directly amenable to Cauchy–Schwarz, to scaling arguments and to positivity manipulations. Essentially every subsequent cone computation is carried out in this form.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypotheses are placed on $v$ or $\vartheta$: the vector $v$ is *not* assumed to be a unit vector, and $\vartheta$ is an arbitrary real. The equivalence is thus a definitional unfolding valid in complete generality, and the geometric reading as an angle condition is only accurate when $\|v\| = 1$ and $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$.
